<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hadith</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hadith are reports of the sayings, actions and approvals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, transmitted through chains of narrators. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> they form a principal source of Islamic law and practice. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, the Prophet's example guided the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>' approach to government and law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hadith.jpg" alt="Hadith" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bakkouz at Arabic Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadith_Books.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Arabic word hadith means a report or an account of an event. It refers to the oral tradition of anecdotes containing the purported words, actions and silent approvals of Muhammad or his immediate circle, the companions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahl-al-bayt">Ahl al-Bayt</a> in Shia Islam. The traditions first circulated in early discussions after Muhammad's death, often without formal attribution. They were not promptly written down in his lifetime or immediately after his death, and the majority were documented several centuries later, some 200 to 300 years afterwards in Sunni sources and 400 to 500 in Shia books. From the 7th to the 10th centuries scholars called muhaddiths compiled them, organising them first by the primary authority and then by subject.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Each hadith has two parts: the text of the report, the matn, and the chain of narrators, the isnad, a chronological list of those who heard and repeated it. Scholars from the eighth century onward have repeated that the isnad is part of the religion. Biographical analysis examines each narrator's dates, teachers, religiosity and travels to judge reliability, and a report carried by many independent chains is called mutawatir. Reports are graded as sahih (authentic), hasan (good) or daif (weak), and fabricated reports are classed as mawdu.</p><p>Different branches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> refer to different collections. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> the canonical collections are six books, of which Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim generally have the highest status. Twelver Shia use the Four Books, beginning with Kitab al-Kafi, the Ismaili Shia use the Da'a'im al-Islam, and the Ibadi branch uses the Tartib al-Musnad. Shia collections give preference to hadith attributed to Muhammad's family and close companions. Hadith are part of the Sunnah, the body of traditions and practices, and a significant portion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> rules is derived from them rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Hadith gained their standing as the second authority after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> over centuries. Some groups have questioned or rejected them, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, the Mu'tazila and certain early jurists, and Quranists regard the collections as historical sources to be assessed critically rather than as binding teaching. Western critical scholarship rejects the authenticity of hadith as an accurate record of the historical Muhammad, and some scholars argue that the corpus does not reflect the seventh century but projects back the legal, theological and political conflicts of the 8th to 10th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hadith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The legal system of this period drew on Quranic injunctions, remembered prophetic practice, later systematised as <b>hadith</b>, and the reasoned judgement (ijtihad) of the companions, without the elaborated schools of jurisprudence that developed under the Abbasids.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The second source of Islamic teaching is the sunna, the example of the prophet, known through the <b>hadith</b>, reports of his words and deeds transmitted by chains of narrators.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>These sought reform through a direct return to the Quran and <b>hadith</b> and blamed Muslim decline on the infiltration of local beliefs and practices.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hadiths</b> serve as further guidance for most Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/work/quran">Quran · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The original complex consisted of the mosque, four madrasas, a primary school, a medical school, a hospital, a public kitchen serving food to the poor, a caravanserai, public baths, a school for the study of <b>hadith</b> and rows of shops, and many of these structures still exist.</blockquote><a href="/work/suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The ulama were the scholars of the Qur’an, the traditions of the Prophet (<b>hadith</b>) and religious law.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
